# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT pinned, recorded so a later reader does not "fix" them:
#
#   ~/Documents/workspaces/agent
#     Holds a dark-factory release branch by design, not by drift.
#
#   every MAIN checkout (trading, sm-octopus, openbrain, ~/.claude, …)
#     A pin here refuses `git worktree add`, the documented way to branch.
#     Measured 2026-10-12: during the add, git runs this hook at the INVOKING
#     checkout — `PWD` and `rev-parse --show-toplevel` both resolve the parent
#     and `GIT_DIR` is unset — while the HEAD line it emits names the NEW
#     worktree. The two are indistinguishable from inside the hook, so a pinned
#     main checkout could not create a worktree at all. Main checkouts are
#     covered instead by ~/.claude/hooks/deny-branch-switch-in-main-checkout.sh,
#     which sees Claude's own commands.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What a pin does NOT cover — measured 2026-10-12:
#
#   A pin guards an EXISTING tree against being switched away. It does not guard
#   the CREATION of one. The same blind spot that keeps main checkouts
#   unpinnable means this succeeds:
#
#       git worktree remove ../trading-dev
#       git worktree add ../trading-dev -b feat/x
#
#   The hook judges the invoking checkout, never the new tree, so the pinned
#   path comes back on the wrong branch. Verified: a path pinned to `other` was
#   created on `third`, rc=0.
#
#   So a deploy worktree can be rebuilt wrong even though it can never be
#   switched wrong. Closing that needs a check where the damage actually lands —
#   `make buca` from the deploy worktree — and is not done here. Until it is, do
#   not read a pin as a guarantee that the tree holds its branch.
